<h2>Where Section 25 sits in the maintenance landscape</h2>
<p>Matrimonial finance runs on several parallel tracks: interim maintenance under Section 24 HMA while the case is pending, the summary remedy under Section 144 of the BNSS (formerly Section 125 CrPC), monetary relief under the Domestic Violence Act, and personal-law remedies. Section 25 is the terminal provision — it operates when the marriage is being or has been judicially dissolved or otherwise adjudicated, and it is designed to produce the final allocation. Amounts received under the other provisions are typically adjusted so that maintenance is not duplicated across forums; the affidavit-of-assets regime now standard in Delhi ensures both sides\' finances are on the record before quantum is fixed.</p>

<h2>What courts weigh</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Income and property</strong><p>The respondent\'s income and other property, and the applicant\'s own — pay slips, tax returns, bank statements and the asset affidavits now required in matrimonial litigation.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Conduct of the parties</strong><p>Section 25 expressly includes the conduct of the parties — the one maintenance provision where matrimonial conduct is a statutory factor, though it moderates quantum rather than erasing genuine need.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Standard of living</strong><p>The benchmark is the standard enjoyed during the marriage, tempered by the reality that one household\'s income must now run two.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Duration and stage of life</strong><p>Length of the marriage, age, health, earning capacity sacrificed for the family, and responsibility for children all shape both form and figure.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Quantum in practice</h2>
<p>No formula is prescribed, and the reported range is wide because the inputs are. In recent practice, courts fixing one-time alimony in middle-income cases commonly work from a multiple of annual maintenance needs, adjusted for the assets each side retains, the children\'s future costs (which are the children\'s right, independent of the spouse\'s claim), and the payer\'s realistic liquidity — an order the payer cannot perform is an invitation to execution litigation. Where income is concealed, courts attribute earning capacity from lifestyle, qualifications and past earnings; suppression in the asset affidavit is treated severely. The guiding idea is neither punishment nor windfall: the dependent spouse should not be left destitute by the dissolution, and the paying spouse should not be reduced to it either.</p>

<h2>Variation, rescission and enforcement</h2>
<p>Subsections (2) and (3) keep the order alive to changed reality. Variation requires a demonstrated change of circumstances of either party — the provision looks forward, so pre-decree facts that could have been argued earlier rarely qualify. Rescission is available on remarriage of the recipient and on the conduct ground in the statute. Enforcement follows the ordinary machinery for decrees: execution under the CPC, with attachment of salary and property available, and the Family Court\'s own enforcement powers. A well-drafted Section 25 order anticipates enforcement — specifying payment dates, security where the payer\'s income is informal, and consequences of default — because the quality of the drafting determines whether the "final" settlement actually ends the litigation.</p>

<h2>Strategic notes for both sides</h2>
<p>For claimants: plead the marital standard of living with particulars, file a complete asset affidavit, and decide early whether finality (lump sum) or security of income (periodic) serves the client better — the answer differs for a young professional and a sixty-year-old homemaker. For payers: a credible, documented disclosure is the strongest quantum argument available, because courts punish concealment with adverse inferences; and a realistic lump-sum offer at the right moment often buys what years of contest cannot — a clean end.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This is a general explainer on Section 25 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 and is not legal advice. Parallel provisions exist under the Special Marriage Act and other personal laws, and the interaction of multiple maintenance orders in a given case requires specific advice.</p>
</div>
